1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:03,199
A robot arm with every motor in its base.

2
00:00:03,199 --> 00:00:09,400
Belts, push rods and parallelograms drive the joints, and the gripper stays level in every pose.

3
00:00:10,350 --> 00:00:13,079
Laying out those linkages by hand is painful:

4
00:00:13,079 --> 00:00:16,718
link lengths, dead points, parts that crash into each other.

5
00:00:17,668 --> 00:00:23,503
Today I'll show you how to design one with SOMA AI by simply describing it, and then watch it move.

6
00:00:23,503 --> 00:00:24,918
No CAD knowledge needed.

7
00:00:25,868 --> 00:00:26,614
We use SOMA.

8
00:00:26,614 --> 00:00:30,718
It's free and open source, and you can download it at somacad.app.

9
00:00:31,668 --> 00:00:33,209
SOMA itself is free.

10
00:00:33,209 --> 00:00:35,907
For SOMA AI, you bring your own AI:

11
00:00:35,907 --> 00:00:38,758
a ChatGPT subscription or an API key.

12
00:00:40,208 --> 00:00:42,615
We start with a new model, Robot arm.

13
00:00:42,615 --> 00:00:48,146
The arm should get real NEMA 17 steppers, so first I import a NEMA 17 as a STEP file.

14
00:00:48,146 --> 00:00:50,098
It lands in the inputs folder.

15
00:00:53,041 --> 00:00:55,908
You can view a STEP file by clicking it in the file browser:

16
00:00:55,908 --> 00:00:57,150
it shows up in the viewer.

17
00:01:00,108 --> 00:01:02,045
Everything is prepared.

18
00:01:02,045 --> 00:01:04,992
Now we design the arm with SOMA AI:

19
00:01:04,992 --> 00:01:09,118
I click its tab, and pick the model, GPT-6.1 Sol.

20
00:01:13,108 --> 00:01:16,284
For a design like this, I set the effort to High:

21
00:01:16,284 --> 00:01:18,358
it thinks longer before it acts.

22
00:01:20,808 --> 00:01:23,160
Then I describe the arm in plain words:

23
00:01:23,160 --> 00:01:35,282
a desktop arm like a Dobot or uArm, all motors in the base, the imported NEMA 17 for all three, push rods to the elbow, a parallelogram that keeps the gripper level, and about 250 millimeters of reach.

24
00:01:35,282 --> 00:01:38,298
And it should ask me first if anything is unclear.

25
00:01:42,141 --> 00:01:42,837
And it does:

26
00:01:42,837 --> 00:01:45,967
two questions at once, each with a recommended answer.

27
00:01:45,967 --> 00:01:46,662
I take them:

28
00:01:46,662 --> 00:01:52,111
a mounting plate for my own gripper for now, and timing belts to gear down the two arm motors.

29
00:01:54,208 --> 00:01:56,897
Then it works on its own, for almost an hour.

30
00:01:56,897 --> 00:02:06,818
It lays out the links, the push rods and the belts, places the three motors, replaces screws that were too long, and checks the arm in ten poses for parts that touch.

31
00:02:07,768 --> 00:02:08,617
Here's the arm:

32
00:02:08,617 --> 00:02:09,184
238 parts.

33
00:02:09,184 --> 00:02:17,338
The structure tree groups them, from the fixed base and the rotating base up to the shoulder, the elbow drive, the leveling links and the wrist.

34
00:02:18,288 --> 00:02:21,542
Make the two base groups see-through, and the motors show:

35
00:02:21,542 --> 00:02:23,618
three copies of the imported NEMA 17.

36
00:02:24,968 --> 00:02:28,424
One sits under the turntable and turns the whole arm with a belt.

37
00:02:28,424 --> 00:02:34,538
The other two ride on the turntable, back to back, and drive the shoulder and the elbow, also through timing belts.

38
00:02:36,101 --> 00:02:38,042
From the side, you see the linkage:

39
00:02:38,042 --> 00:02:43,642
paired push rods drive the elbow from the base, and two parallelograms keep the mounting plate level.

40
00:02:48,601 --> 00:02:50,237
But does the linkage actually work?

41
00:02:50,237 --> 00:02:53,931
Let's ask for an animation of the full range of motion, with a collision check.

42
00:03:00,301 --> 00:03:06,251
SOMA AI writes the motion and checks every pose with the whole assembly, motors and screws included.

43
00:03:06,251 --> 00:03:08,809
That finds contacts its first check missed:

44
00:03:08,809 --> 00:03:14,104
the elbow axle touches the leveling links at steep angles, and screw heads hit the drive.

45
00:03:14,104 --> 00:03:18,031
So it moves the screws, and narrows the shoulder and elbow travel.

46
00:03:18,981 --> 00:03:21,496
The animation is in the Animation tab:

47
00:03:21,496 --> 00:03:23,084
pick it, and press play.

48
00:03:23,084 --> 00:03:24,871
Here it runs twice as fast.

49
00:03:27,748 --> 00:03:30,055
The base turns 150 degrees each way.

50
00:03:30,055 --> 00:03:35,310
Then the shoulder sweeps from 25 to 55 degrees, and the elbow bends from 35 to 80.

51
00:03:35,310 --> 00:03:41,398
The push rods and the parallelograms follow, and the mounting plate stays level the whole time.

52
00:03:50,648 --> 00:03:51,567
And the report:

53
00:03:51,567 --> 00:03:57,818
no collisions at 441 positions, checked with the whole assembly, motors, bearings and screws included.

54
00:03:59,581 --> 00:04:01,436
Now a change, in words again:

55
00:04:01,436 --> 00:04:05,851
more reach, 300 millimeters, and a simple gripper with a small servo.

56
00:04:05,851 --> 00:04:09,370
Both jaws should move, and stay parallel to each other.

57
00:04:15,214 --> 00:04:16,440
Again it asks first:

58
00:04:16,440 --> 00:04:21,039
where to measure the reach, which servo, and how wide the jaws should open.

59
00:04:21,039 --> 00:04:26,864
I pick the reach to the center of the grip, a generic 9-gram servo for now, and 30 millimeters.

60
00:04:28,981 --> 00:04:30,866
Then it works for almost an hour:

61
00:04:30,866 --> 00:04:40,231
it lengthens the arm, so that the center of the grip reaches 300 millimeters, builds the gripper, extends the animation, and checks everything again for collisions.

62
00:04:41,181 --> 00:04:42,166
Here's the gripper.

63
00:04:42,166 --> 00:04:45,951
The micro servo sits under the wrist plate and turns a small gear on top.

64
00:04:46,901 --> 00:04:49,978
Two racks mesh with that gear from opposite sides.

65
00:04:49,978 --> 00:04:57,178
Each rack carries one jaw, so both jaws move the same distance in opposite directions, and they always stay parallel.

66
00:05:00,748 --> 00:05:04,004
SOMA AI also made a separate animation of the gripper:

67
00:05:04,004 --> 00:05:06,718
it opens to 30 millimeters, and closes again.

68
00:05:12,448 --> 00:05:15,346
And the arm's animation still works with the longer arm.

69
00:05:15,346 --> 00:05:20,418
The collision check passes again, and the 14 builds at the ends of every parameter range pass too.

70
00:05:23,848 --> 00:05:24,889
First the base turns.

71
00:05:24,889 --> 00:05:30,538
Then the shoulder and the elbow sweep to their limits, and then both move together, while the gripper stays level.

72
00:05:39,448 --> 00:05:41,533
Near the end, the camera moves in:

73
00:05:41,533 --> 00:05:45,457
the servo turns the gear, and both jaws open and close together.

74
00:05:46,907 --> 00:05:50,439
The joint angles are parameters, and so are the reach and the jaw opening.

75
00:05:50,439 --> 00:05:52,157
You find them in the Parameters tab.

76
00:05:55,507 --> 00:05:57,449
Shoulder down to 35 degrees:

77
00:05:57,449 --> 00:05:59,876
SOMA rebuilds the arm in that pose.

78
00:06:02,541 --> 00:06:03,911
Elbow to 45 degrees:

79
00:06:03,911 --> 00:06:08,911
the push rods and the parallelograms follow, and the gripper stays level.

80
00:06:10,074 --> 00:06:12,124
The base turns by 45 degrees.

81
00:06:17,107 --> 00:06:19,157
And the jaws open to 30 millimeters.

82
00:06:24,141 --> 00:06:26,671
Reset all brings the arm back to its home pose.

83
00:06:30,774 --> 00:06:32,544
Now look closer at the gripper.

84
00:06:32,544 --> 00:06:35,114
It opens and closes, but it isn't usable yet:

85
00:06:35,114 --> 00:06:41,624
the jaws barely reach past the wrist, and every gripping force goes through thin rods into the racks and the gear.

86
00:06:46,974 --> 00:06:52,304
So I start a new conversation with SOMA AI, again with GPT-6.1 Sol and High effort.

87
00:06:54,874 --> 00:06:56,936
So I ask for a new gripper from scratch:

88
00:06:56,936 --> 00:07:04,204
the jaws run in their own guide that takes all the forces, the gear and the racks only make the movement, and the jaws reach further forward.

89
00:07:10,841 --> 00:07:12,450
SOMA AI asks one thing first:

90
00:07:12,450 --> 00:07:17,944
the guides can take the bending and lifting loads, but something still has to hold the grip closed.

91
00:07:17,944 --> 00:07:21,051
The servo should do that, so I pick the servo-held grip.

92
00:07:22,001 --> 00:07:23,854
An hour and ten minutes of work.

93
00:07:23,854 --> 00:07:27,153
SOMA AI throws the old gripper away and builds a new one:

94
00:07:27,153 --> 00:07:30,974
a guide housing, two jaw carriages, separate racks and a new gear.

95
00:07:30,974 --> 00:07:33,811
Then it checks the motion of the whole arm again.

96
00:07:34,761 --> 00:07:36,291
Here is the new gripper.

97
00:07:36,291 --> 00:07:38,586
The jaws now run in a guide housing:

98
00:07:38,586 --> 00:07:42,794
each jaw carriage has two T-shaped shoes, held in by a bolted lid.

99
00:07:42,794 --> 00:07:46,491
The housing takes the bending, lifting and twisting loads.

100
00:07:47,441 --> 00:07:54,291
The fingers reach 48 millimeters beyond the housing, 40 millimeters further forward than before, with flat parallel faces at the tips.

101
00:07:55,241 --> 00:07:59,229
With the housing made see-through, you can see the gear and the two racks:

102
00:07:59,229 --> 00:08:00,522
they only move the jaws.

103
00:08:00,522 --> 00:08:05,210
The racks are bolted on separately, and as I chose, they still carry the closing force.

104
00:08:06,160 --> 00:08:08,086
One thing it decided on its own:

105
00:08:08,086 --> 00:08:14,285
to keep the reach at 300 millimeters to the grip center, it made the upper arm and the forearm shorter.

106
00:08:14,285 --> 00:08:15,970
The arm is now more compact.

107
00:08:17,328 --> 00:08:20,737
And both jaws still move parallel, driven by the servo.

108
00:08:21,687 --> 00:08:23,378
The new gripper is heavier:

109
00:08:23,378 --> 00:08:29,016
about 136 grams of printed parts, so little of the 200 gram target is left for the object.

110
00:08:29,016 --> 00:08:31,897
The real servo and the grip still need a test.

111
00:08:32,847 --> 00:08:35,152
This was a simple example, made in a short time.

112
00:08:35,152 --> 00:08:38,177
With more work and planning, you can build something like this:

113
00:08:39,127 --> 00:08:43,657
a robot dog, designed earlier in SOMA together with an agent, over several sessions.

114
00:08:46,780 --> 00:08:51,790
Each leg has three joints, and each one has its own motor with a 6:1 planetary gearbox.

115
00:08:52,740 --> 00:08:58,230
The motor turns the sun gear, the planets roll inside the fixed ring gear, and the carrier turns the leg.

116
00:08:59,180 --> 00:09:03,191
Describe it, check it, change it, all in plain words.

117
00:09:03,191 --> 00:09:05,689
Get SOMA for free at somacad.app.
